 web_fetch, connections, connect_service, http_request, disconnect_service, todo_write, ask_user, task, skill, save_skill, generate_image, schedule, svc_Telegram_*, svc_Weather_*.\n\nNo net_* tools. So I cannot directly call net_info etc. \n\nOptions:\n1. Use the `task` tool to delegate to a subagent that has these tools (it-admin or general). The it-admin subagent is described as \"Системный администратор сервера WORK — состояние сервера (диски, память, службы, процессы), файлы ОС вне WORK, инвентаризация локальной сети, регистрация принтеров/сканеров/1С в /SERVICES/LAN, диагностика «что сломалось». Только для администраторов WORK.\"\n\nSo the it-admin subagent has the net_* tools! I should delegate to it-admin.\n\nLet me delegate the LAN inventory to the it-admin subagent. That's the cleanest approach.\n\nActually, let me think about whether I should just do it myself. The net_* tools are guarded for admins and the user is ADMIN. But they're not in my tool list. The subagent it-admin is specifically for this. Let me delegate.\n\nLet me write a self-contained prompt for the it-admin subagent to do the LAN inventory following the skill, and report back findings. I'll ask it to do discovery (net_info, net_discover, net_candidates) which don't require confirmation, and report. For net_scan which requires confirmation, I should let it report what it found and what it recommends, and I'll relay to the user.\n\nActually the subagent runs with the user's privileges and confirmations. Let me have it do the read-only discovery first and report, and ask for confirmation before scanning.\n\nLet me delegate to it-admin.\n",
