              "reasoning": "I've understood the toolset. The net_* tools are agent tools, and they're exposed via the MCP pool. However, in this environment, I only have the following tools: ls, read, find, access, write, call. The net_* tools are not directly available to me.\n\nWait — the system prompt says \"Инструменты `os_*`/`net_*` есть только у администратора: нет их — сообщи, что поручение требует роли ADMIN в корне WORK.\" (The `os_*`/`net_*` tools are only available to the administrator: if you don't have them — report that the task requires the ADMIN role at the root of WORK.)\n\nI'm operating in the USER role (the user selected it for this task). The user is an administrator (has ADMIN, BOSS roles in /BASE/direction/sales), but my session role is USER. The net_* tools are \"guarded() only for WORK administrators\" — meaning they're only exposed to the root ADMIN.\n\nHmm, but wait. Let me check how the tools are exposed. The available tools are: ls, read, find, access, write, call. There's no net_info, net_discover, net_candidates, and no os_* either.\n\nSo I literally can't call net_info, net_discover, net_candidates. The instructions say: \"Если сети нет или доступов нет — сообщи об этом прямо.\" (If there's no network or no access — report it directly.)\n\nBut wait — maybe I can call them via `call`? The `call` tool invokes server-side methods of WORK elements. The net tools are agent tools, not element methods. Let me check how the agent tools are wired up — maybe there's a way to call them via the MCP pool. Let me look at sources/modules/agent/mcp-pool.js and index.js to see how tools are exposed.\n\nActually, let me think about this. The `call` tool: \"Вызвать серверный метод элемента WORK (см. schema): path + method + args. Для чтения, записи и бизнес-операций классов, для которых нет отдельного инструмента.\" (Invoke the server-side method of a WORK element (see schema): path + method + args. For reading, writing, and business operations of classes that don't have a separate tool.)\n\nThe net_* tools are defined in netTools in sources/modules/agent/tools/net.js. They're probably exposed to the agent via the MCP pool. But in this session, I don't have them as tools. The system prompt explicitly says: \"Инструменты os_*/net_* есть только у администратора: нет их — сообщи, что поручение требует роли ADMIN в корне WORK.\" (The os_*/net_* tools are only available to the administrator: if you don't have them — report that the task requires the ADMIN role at the root of WORK.)\n\nSo the correct behavior is: report that the net_* tools are not available in this session (role USER), and that the task needs to be run in the ADMIN role at the root of WORK.\n\nBut wait — before jumping to conclusions, let me double-check. Maybe the tools are available via `call` on some element. Let me check the agent's index.js to see how netTools are registered, and whether there's a server-side method that can be called.\n\nLet me look at sources/modules/agent/index.js around line 179, and mcp-pool.js.\n\nAlso, I should check the following:\n1. /SERVICES/LAN — does it exist? From the ls of /, SERVICES contains ArgoCD, DuckDuckGo, SearXNG, Telegram, Weather, Yandex, readme.md. No LAN folder. So nothing is registered in /SERVICES/LAN.\n2. #system/os.json — the config that includes subnets. Where is #system? Probably at the root of the WORK tree, hidden. Let me check with find or ls. The test wrote '#system/os.json' to the root of the tree. Let me try reading /#system/os.json.\n\nLet me also check the candidates store: #system/lan/candidates.json.\n\nLet me do these reads in parallel:\n- read /#system/os.json\n- read /#system/lan/candidates.json\n- ls /SERVICES\n- read sources/modules/agent/index.js (to check tool registration)\n\nActually, #system might not be visible via ls (it's a hidden system folder). Let me try reading it directly.\n\nLet me also check how the tools are exposed — maybe there's a \"call\" to the agent's MCP. Let me